1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,043
Lesson 4.7.

2
00:00:02,423 --> 00:00:04,930
Boltzmann machines and
their learning rule.

3
00:00:06,050 --> 00:00:09,669
A Hopfield network stores
patterns we give it explicitly.

4
00:00:10,048 --> 00:00:14,550
In 1985, Ackley, Hinton and
Sejnowski took the next step:

5
00:00:14,550 --> 00:00:18,815
a spin network at positive
temperature that learns, by

6
00:00:18,815 --> 00:00:22,685
itself, a probability
distribution from examples.

7
00:00:23,065 --> 00:00:24,940
They called it a Boltzmann machine.

8
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:30,052
It is a generative model: after
training, its samples look like the data.

9
00:00:31,183 --> 00:00:32,461
What does learning mean?

10
00:00:32,841 --> 00:00:35,909
That the model's statistics
match those of the data.

11
00:00:36,289 --> 00:00:38,731
In particular, that
every pair of neurons is

12
00:00:38,731 --> 00:00:41,672
correlated the same way in
the model and in the data.

13
00:00:42,052 --> 00:00:43,675
The rule has two phases.

14
00:00:44,055 --> 00:00:46,430
In the wake phase we look at the data and

15
00:00:46,430 --> 00:00:49,558
strengthen the connections
between neurons that agree.

16
00:00:49,938 --> 00:00:52,750
In the sleep phase we let the model dream

17
00:00:52,750 --> 00:00:55,767
freely and weaken what
agrees in its dreams.

18
00:00:56,147 --> 00:01:00,945
When dreams and data have the
same correlations, learning stops.

19
00:01:02,050 --> 00:01:08,971
Formally, we maximize the log-likelihood
of the data under p ∝ e^(−βE).

20
00:01:09,351 --> 00:01:13,277
The derivative with respect to a
coupling has two terms: one from

21
00:01:13,277 --> 00:01:16,660
the data energy and another
from the partition function.

22
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:25,025
The result is ∂ℒ/∂J_ij = β(⟨s_i
s_j⟩_data − ⟨s_i s_j⟩_model).

23
00:01:25,405 --> 00:01:27,917
For the fields, the
difference of the means.

24
00:01:28,297 --> 00:01:31,195
It is equivalent to
minimizing the KL divergence

25
00:01:31,195 --> 00:01:33,972
between the data
distribution and the model's.

26
00:01:35,083 --> 00:01:40,393
A machine with only visible neurons learns
pairwise correlations, nothing more.

27
00:01:40,773 --> 00:01:43,703
To capture higher-order
structure we add hidden

28
00:01:43,703 --> 00:01:46,259
neurons, which do not appear in the data.

29
00:01:46,639 --> 00:01:50,413
In the wake phase we clamp the
visible ones to an example and sample

30
00:01:50,413 --> 00:01:53,576
the hidden ones; in the sleep
phase everything runs free.

31
00:01:53,956 --> 00:01:58,961
The rule is the same, but now the data
correlations also require sampling.

32
00:02:00,083 --> 00:02:03,377
Let's train a machine with
five visible neurons on two

33
00:02:03,377 --> 00:02:06,609
thousand samples generated
by an unknown Ising model.

34
00:02:06,989 --> 00:02:11,575
We use exact gradients, computed by
enumerating the thirty-two states.

35
00:02:11,955 --> 00:02:17,444
The KL divergence to the true
model starts at 1.5, drops

36
00:02:17,444 --> 00:02:22,442
to 0.08 in ten steps and
to 0.002 in three hundred.

37
00:02:22,822 --> 00:02:26,912
The learned couplings
approach the true ones:

38
00:02:26,912 --> 00:02:30,638
the maximum error goes from 0.76 to 0.06.

39
00:02:31,750 --> 00:02:34,017
In a real model we cannot enumerate.

40
00:02:34,397 --> 00:02:37,843
Every learning step
needs equilibrium model

41
00:02:37,843 --> 00:02:41,530
correlations, that is,
a full Monte Carlo run.

42
00:02:41,909 --> 00:02:45,069
On digital hardware that
is very expensive, which is

43
00:02:45,069 --> 00:02:47,985
why Boltzmann machines
were sidelined for years.

44
00:02:48,365 --> 00:02:51,869
But a pbit chip produces
those samples natively: the

45
00:02:51,869 --> 00:02:55,036
sleep phase is simply
letting the hardware run.

46
00:02:55,417 --> 00:02:58,965
It is one of the central motivations
of thermodynamic computing.

47
00:03:00,083 --> 00:03:05,034
Today, the natural scheme is hybrid:
the thermodynamic chip supplies the

48
00:03:05,034 --> 00:03:09,503
model correlations and a digital
processor updates the couplings.

49
00:03:09,883 --> 00:03:13,711
In the next lesson we will
see restricted machines and

50
00:03:13,711 --> 00:03:17,821
contrastive divergence, which
make training practical, and

51
00:03:17,821 --> 00:03:21,719
in unit nine, denoising models
built from these pieces.

52
00:03:22,850 --> 00:03:28,151
Frequent mistakes: using model samples
that are not at equilibrium, forgetting the

53
00:03:28,151 --> 00:03:32,676
field update, expecting higher-order
structure without hidden neurons,

54
00:03:33,056 --> 00:03:35,475
and using learning rates so large that the

55
00:03:35,475 --> 00:03:37,894
sleep phase cannot keep up with the model.

56
00:03:39,017 --> 00:03:43,294
In summary: a Boltzmann machine
is an Ising model that learns.

57
00:03:43,674 --> 00:03:48,404
Its rule, ΔJ_ij ∝ ⟨s_i
s_j⟩_data − ⟨s_i s_j⟩_model,

58
00:03:48,404 --> 00:03:52,763
matches the model's
correlations to the data's.

59
00:03:53,143 --> 00:03:56,495
The sleep phase needs
equilibrium samples, which are

60
00:03:56,495 --> 00:03:59,589
expensive digitally and
native on pbit hardware.

61
00:04:00,717 --> 00:04:05,126
To practice: derive the derivative
of the log partition function.

62
00:04:05,506 --> 00:04:08,274
Show the equivalence
with the KL divergence.

63
00:04:08,654 --> 00:04:10,665
Explain why the gradient vanishes.

64
00:04:11,045 --> 00:04:13,337
And count the states for thirty neurons.
